  "response_text": "1. Create a budget: Start by creating a detailed budget that includes all your expenses, including rent, utilities, groceries, and any other necessary expenses. This will help you identify areas where you can cut back and save money.\n\n2. Negotiate with creditors: If you're struggling to make payments on your debts, it's important to negotiate with your creditors. You can ask for a lower interest rate, a shorter payment period, or a more flexible repayment plan. Be prepared to provide proof of your financial situation, such as a recent paycheck or bank statement.\n\n3. Cut back on non-essential expenses: Look for areas where you can cut back on non-essential expenses. This could include dining out, subscriptions, or luxury items. By reducing your spending on these items, you can free up more money to pay off your debts.\n\n4. Increase your income: If possible, try to increase your income by taking on a part-time job or selling items you no longer need. This will help you earn more money and make it easier to pay off your debts.\n\n5. Seek help from a financial advisor: If you're struggling to manage your finances, consider seeking help from a financial advisor. They can provide you with personalized advice and guidance on how to manage your debts and improve your financial situation.\n\n6. Consider bankruptcy: If you're facing financial difficulties and are unable to pay off your debts, you may consider filing for bankruptcy. This can help you eliminate your debts and give you a fresh start. However, it's important to consult with a bankruptcy attorney to understand the process and potential consequences.",
